1088 0 obj
<<
/Title (Specific Elul customs include the daily sounding of the shofar as a call to repentance. Each weekday morning, the shofar sounds as a call to repentance, urging increased study, prayer, and charity. The Chasidic Master, the Baal Shem Tov instituted the custom of reciting three additional chapters of Psalms each day, from the 1st of Elul until Yom Kippur.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 917 0 R
/Prev 1086 0 R
/Next 1090 0 R
>>
endobj
1089 0 obj
<<
/Title (The anthology includes four Rebbe-based articles: one on Elul's history as a spiritual refuge, one on its astrological sign and its parallel to the marriage between God and Israel, and two exploring Rabbi Schneur Zalman of Liadi's parable of the king in the field.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 917 0 R
/Prev 1091 0 R
/Next 1087 0 R
>>
endobj
1090 0 obj
<<
/Title (Elul is also the recommended time to have one's tefillin and mezuzahs checked by an accredited scribe to ensure they are in good condition and fit for use.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 917 0 R
/Prev 1088 0 R
/Next 1091 0 R
>>
endobj
1091 0 obj
<<
/Title (From the beginning of Elul and throughout the High Holiday season, we include the blessing "May you be inscribed and sealed for a good year" \(Leshanah tovah tikateiv veteichateim\) in letters and greetings to one another. Traditional greetings wish others a good inscription for the new year, and  Selichot \(penitential\) prayers begin in Elul's final week.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 917 0 R
/Prev 1090 0 R
/Next 1089 0 R
>>
endobj
